{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## The evidence set this ticket was waiting for now EXISTS — and it changes the question\n\nThe plan reads \"accumulate a representative set of real advisory review outcomes over time\",\nwhich scans as blocked on the passage of time. It is not. Measured from the ticket store\n2026-08-05, across all 3,299 ticket dirs.\n\n## Sampling window and method (AC1)\n\nTwo distinct numbers; conflating them is the easy mistake:\n\n- **51 review-result events MENTION `project.review-phase-boundaries`** — reviews where the\n  criterion was in scope, carried in the registry/checklist.\n- **6 contain an actual FINDING citing it**, across 6 distinct tickets.\n\nFire rate ~12% of in-scope reviews.\n\n## Impact distribution — the decisive number\n\n    impact 0.0  ->  4 findings\n    impact 0.4  ->  1 finding\n    impact 0.5  ->  1 finding\n    -----------------------------------\n    max observed 0.5   |   blocking threshold 0.90\n\n**No finding has ever come within 0.4 of the threshold.** That reframes this ticket's question.\nFlipping `blocking_enabled` at 0.90 would change NOTHING — no historical finding would have\nblocked. The real question is whether 0.90 is the right threshold given an observed impact\nceiling of 0.5.\n\n## Finding quality — no obvious false positive in the sample\n\nSpecific and substantively correct. Verbatim:\n\n- 1eee: \"The new ac-satisfiability rubric — explicitly applied by the Pass-1 finder — instructs\n  it to assign severity (MAJOR/MINOR), which is the deterministic outcome work owned by Pass-3.\"\n- 7cf9: \"Changed G7 rubric directs the discovery-phase reviewer to make a dedup/drop decision ...\n  which is deterministic-decision (Pass-3) work.\"\n- 8cea: \"The new no-file-impact Pass-1 criterion rubric directs the evaluator to decide the\n  review outcome (Return PASS), which is Pass-3 deterministic-decision work.\"\n\nEach names a concrete rubric and the boundary it crosses. The criterion is doing its job.\n\n## Options (AC2)\n\nThe criterion is USEFUL but consistently SUB-THRESHOLD:\n\n1. **Keep advisory, keep the threshold.** Findings are real but low-impact by their own scoring.\n   Honest, costs nothing, changes nothing.\n2. **Keep advisory, LOWER the threshold** so 0.4-0.5 findings surface more prominently without\n   blocking. Fixes the mismatch between observed impact and configured threshold, no author\n   friction.\n3. **Promote to blocking AND lower to ~0.4.** Only this blocks anything. Volume is low (6 over\n   the window) so author impact would be small — but it makes a phase-boundary violation a hard\n   stop on six data points, none rated above 0.5 by its own scorer.\n\n## Caveats — do not over-read this\n\n- 6 findings is a small sample. One high-impact miss would change the picture.\n- I judged the findings \"appear correct\" by READING them. That is not the same as confirming with\n  each author that the finding was actionable, which is what AC2's \"false positives\" and \"author\n  impact\" actually ask for.\n- Impact scores are self-reported by the reviewer that produced them, so \"max 0.5\" is the\n  criterion's own view of its severity, not an independent judgement.\n\nAC3 (any routing change lands through an independently reviewed ticket and Gerrit change) is\nunaffected and still applies to whichever option is chosen."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785919546866926001,"uuid":"ad2f1105-3c39-4cad-b84b-1ba12bbf9cd6"}